# =============================================================================
# .gitignore — protect local developer SECRETS from being committed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Ignores the local environment file `.env` (and its common local
#       variants) while keeping the committed placeholder template `.env.example`
#       tracked.
#
# WHY:  The containerized dev environment reads real credentials
#       (W5BASE_DB_PASSWORD, MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD, W5BASE_ADMIN_PASSWORD, ...) from
#       `.env`, which docker-compose.yml loads via `env_file:`. Committing `.env`
#       would leak those secrets into version control — exactly the posture the
#       plan forbids ("Never hardcode secrets"; keep real secrets in `.env`,
#       which must NEVER be committed). `.env.example` itself instructs adding
#       `.env` to .gitignore; this file fulfils that instruction so a developer
#       who copies `.env.example` to `.env` and fills in real values has a guard
#       against an accidental `git add .`.
#
# HOW:  Git ignores the patterns below. The `.env.example` (and `.env.sample`)
#       template stays tracked via the explicit negation entries, so onboarding
#       docs and `cp .env.example .env` continue to work.
# =============================================================================

# Local environment file holding REAL secrets — never commit.
.env
# Common local override variants developers create — also never commit.
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Keep the committed, secret-free templates tracked (must NOT be ignored).
!.env.example
!.env.sample
